thanks to skillshare for keeping legal  eagle in the air learn to think like a  lawyer for free for two months by  clicking the link in the description  attorney general william barr wrote a  four-page summary of the mueller report  here are the nine reasons i don't think  it makes any sense  [Music]  hey legal eagles today we're talking  about the bar letter this is not the  mueller report as a lot of people have  reported this is a summary of the  mueller report as conveyed by the  attorney general  the bar letter purports to summarize the  findings of special counsel robert  mueller  and his investigation into the trump  campaign and potential ties to  russian interference with our election  when the bar letter came out on sunday i  like many americans read it cover to  cover  but there were a couple things that  stuck out to me as an attorney that just  didn't make any sense so here are the  nine things that i don't think make any  sense in the bar letter  number one why does the bar letter only  cover two of the three facets of the  mueller report you might recall that  robert mueller was tasked with  investigating three things  a criminal investigation into potential  ties with russia  a criminal investigation into potential  obstruction of justice by the president  and number three a counterintelligence  investigation  into russian interference with the  american election  but the bar letter only deals with two  of the three facets  of the mueller report and doesn't  actually deal with the  counterintelligence portions of  the mueller investigation why is that  information missing  from barr's letter barr could have  easily provided a summary of the  counterintelligence portion of the  investigation  but for some reason he chose not to why  is that information  not there and we also learned that barr  is going to make a  separate summary that can only be given  to the gang of eight instead of the  full intelligence committees of the  house and the senate so  why is there information that can only  be provided to the gang of eight  when a summary even a top level summary  can't be provided in the letter that is  released to congress in general and to  the american people number two  what does barr mean when he says that  the mueller report quote  did not establish end quote a conspiracy  with the russians what does it mean to  not establish  in this context the bar letter quotes a  portion of the mueller report stating  that  the investigation did not establish that  members of the trump campaign conspired  or coordinated  with the russian government in its  election interference activities  the problem is that this phrase can mean  wildly different things  does it mean that there was no evidence  at all or does it mean that there is a  boatload of evidence but there is not  quite enough to meet the standard of  beyond a reasonable  doubt that is necessary for a criminal  prosecution if a prosecutor says that an  investigation  did not establish something it doesn't  mean that that thing did not happen  it doesn't even mean that the prosecutor  doesn't think it happened  it means that the investigation didn't  produce enough information to prove  beyond a reasonable doubt in front of a  jury that that thing  happened so in this particular context  we don't know which of those potential  outcomes there is is it that there is  absolutely no evidence  or that there is evidence but not quite  enough to meet the legal standard for a  criminal prosecution the difference  between those two things  is incredibly large and is very  important as it regards what congress is  supposed to do next with this  investigation number three  why does barr suggest that there can be  no obstruction of justice  when there isn't a conviction for an  underlying crime attorney general barr  suggests in his letter that you cannot  have  a conviction of obstruction of justice  when there is no  underlying criminal act or conviction  for an underlying criminal act  but that's simply not the case a  conviction for an underlying criminal  act  is not necessary for a conviction of  obstruction of justice just talk to  martha stewart  barry bonds bill clinton or kwame  kilpatrick  the last example is especially  illustrative because former detroit  mayor kwame kilpatrick went to jail  for obstruction of justice in 2008 after  he lied under oath about having an  extramarital affair  having an affair is not an illegal act  but it's often embarrassing  especially for politicians and they can  go to jail for obstructing justice to  prevent that information from coming out  this is especially worrying  because barr wrote a now famous perhaps  infamous 19-page memo  laying out a very idiosyncratic view of  obstruction of justice  in the 19-page memo barr opined that  quote the president's motive in removing  comey and commenting on flynn could not  have been  corrupt unless the president and his  campaign were actually guilty of  illegal collusion the vast majority of  legal scholars agree that this is simply  not the case  that even in the absence of an actual  guilty  verdict of illegal collusion or  conspiracy or coordination  that is not necessary as a predicate for  obstructing justice  into that investigation but that brings  me to point number four  why does attorney general barr think  that there was no crime  even taking attorney general barr's  idiosyncratic view of obstruction of  justice at face value  even so there was crime that was found  in the mueller investigation and  throughout the investigations into the  president president trump is effectively  an unindicted co-conspirator  in a felony campaign finance violation  with michael cohen  hiding or at least preventing the  investigation into that  embarrassing affair and illegal conduct  is more than enough reason to obstruct  justice and can give rise to the  corrupt mental state necessary to be  guilty of obstruction of justice  potentially that investigation was  farmed out to the southern district of  new york but even still  that is potential criminal conduct that  could give rise to obstruction of  justice not to mention all the other  convictions of  papadopoulos manafort and flynn so the  question is why would barr  or mueller himself limit the crime at  issue to  the very narrow definition of russian  conspiracy  that was talked about in the bar letter  i don't think that makes any sense which  takes me to point number five  why didn't robert mueller decide the  obstruction issue  the bar letter states that quote after  making a  thorough factual investigation into  these matters the special counsel  considered whether to evaluate the  conduct under department standards  governing prosecution and declination  decisions  but ultimately determined not to make a  traditional prosecutorial judgment  the special counsel therefore did not  draw a conclusion one way or the other  as to whether the examined conduct  constituted obstruction  that doesn't sound like robert mueller  declined to prosecute because of the  facts  or that there wasn't enough evidence but  because of some countervailing legal  issue  perhaps because of the doj policy not to  indict the sitting president but the bar  letter goes on to state that  instead for each of the relevant actions  investigated the report  sets out evidence on both sides of the  question and leaves unresolved  what the special counsel views as  difficult decisions of law and fact  concerning whether the president's  actions and intent could be viewed as  obstruction  to me that doesn't make any sense if the  facts were 50 50 what we call equipoise  in the law  that doesn't meet the standard of beyond  a reasonable doubt when there is some  evidence  but not enough to reach the very high  standard of beyond a reasonable doubt  then a prosecutor declines to prosecute  in fact mueller apparently did exactly  this  with the collusion coordination and  conspiracy investigation  which barr summarizes as finding that  mueller did not find enough evidence to  establish  those crimes so why did robert mueller  punt  on the obstruction question but not on  the collusion  and conspiracy question which brings me  to the next point point number six  why did william barr weigh in on the  obstruction issue william barr  is a recent political appointee by  president trump  the point of the special counsel statute  was for a neutral party to investigate  the executive branch  if robert mueller found enough evidence  to convict  someone for obstruction of justice the  bar letter appears to state  that robert mueller did not decline to  prosecute for obstruction of justice  because there wasn't enough evidence  but because of some other legal issue  then does that mean that robert mueller  intended for william barr to weigh in  on the question or did robert mueller  intend for congress to answer this  question  was it intended to be teed up for  congress to make that decision  declining to make a quote traditional  prosecutorial decision  is not necessarily inviting the  political appointee william barr to make  that decision  and on top of that robert mueller as  special counsel has exclusive  jurisdiction  in this matter why did he decide not to  move forward with  the obstruction prosecution we know that  when robert mueller does not find enough  evidence to prosecute he  makes a decision not to prosecute but  here in the context of obstruction of  justice  robert mueller is refusing to make any  decision whatsoever we will only know  what that means when the full mueller  report  is revealed and on top of that how is it  possible that william barr can make this  decision  in 48 hours when robert mueller couldn't  make the decision  in two years and was william barr even  able to review  all of the underlying evidence in this  case we don't even know how long the  mueller report itself actually is  which brings me to point number seven  whose legal conclusions are these  and are there other legal conclusions  that we haven't seen yet the bar letter  only purports to convey mueller's  top-line findings and does not include  any of the underlying evidence or legal  analysis that undergirds those findings  the bard letter talks about the two  quote principle findings of the mueller  report  but does that mean that there are other  legal findings that mueller concluded  that  are not part of these top-line findings  and as to the legal conclusions that we  have  we don't know whether attorney general  barr concluded that the president did  not obstruct justice as a factual matter  or that it wasn't legally possible for  him to have obstructed justice  again going back to barr's unsolicited  19-page memo  he argued a minority position that a  president effectively cannot obstruct  justice when it comes to using his  article ii  power and barr also argued that the  obstruction of justice statutes don't  apply to the president  because they do not specifically mention  the president if attorney general barr  imputed those minority legal positions  onto robert mueller in the investigation  then it was effectively impossible as a  logical matter  for the president to have conducted  obstruction of justice which brings me  to point number eight  why did barr quote the portion of the  mueller report that states that  the mueller report does not exonerate  the president the bar  letter is potential partisan  whitewashing we don't know what's in the  underlying report  and we don't know how accurate of a  summary the bar letter actually is  it's possible that barr is intentionally  setting the narrative  before the full muller report comes out  but even still  why include this cherry-picked phrase  that is actually detrimental to the  president stating that the mueller  report does not  in fact exonerate the president if a  report or evidence doesn't exonerate  someone from a crime  and you're writing a summary about it  you don't have to include a sentence  that says  the report does not exonerate that  person you can just leave it at the fact  that  the crime was not established or that  the decision was made  not to prosecute you don't have to go to  the extra step and say that it doesn't  exonerate that person it seems like an  odd inclusion in this very  high level summary of the mueller report  which takes me to point number nine  which may be the most baffling thing  about the bar letter and  potentially the mueller report itself  which is that there was a decision made  not to prosecute for obstruction of  justice  but that decision was made without  interviewing the president  was there an effort to actually  interview the president we know that  under past precedent under the clinton  versus jones affair  and also the star investigation into  president clinton  that the president can be made to stand  for an interview  was an effort to get president trump to  stand for an interview made  and on top of that why allow the  president to offer written answers to  written questions  in lieu of an actual oral examination  obstruction of justice requires proving  that the defendant  had a corrupt motive in conducting the  acts  that constitute the obstruction of  justice the best way to determine that  is to interview the defendant now  potentially the defendant can take the  fifth amendment but here  given that the best evidence one way or  the other  is going to come from the president  about his motivations in taking  these different actions why was the  president not  interviewed in this investigation now  that the investigation  is done attorney general barr is going  to have to read through thousands of  pages of the mueller report to determine  what can be publicly released he could  really benefit from a speed reading  class to get through the full muller  report quickly  like silvio mariska's skillshare class  on speed reading which could teach barr  to triple his reading speed in seven  days silvio's class could teach barr how  to read the mueller report at a thousand  words per minute  and at the same time improve his reading  comprehension and retention  at that rate we might actually see the  mueller report before the next  presidential election skillshare is an  online learning community that has over  20  000 classes on everything like lifestyle  design technology business you name it  the first 500 legal eagles will get two  free months of skillshare when you click  on the link below the free premium  membership gives you unlimited access to  must-know topics so you can improve your  skills and learn new things  so click on the link get two free months  and start learning to think like a  lawyer today  it could even help you read the mueller  report and figure out why he refused to  make the call  on obstruction do you agree leave your  objections in the comments  and check out my other real law reviews  over here  where i will see you in court

